{%- comment -%}
  Usage: {% render 'buy-box', product: product, compact: false %}
  compact: true  -> tighter spacing, no product title repeat
  Product-agnostic: every string comes from the product, no hardcoded fallbacks.
  Button says only "Add to Cart" - no price, no spinner. Below 900px the button
  is hidden and the permanent footer bar is the only Add to Cart.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign v = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign prepaid_code = settings.prepaid_discount_code
  assign prepaid_pct = settings.prepaid_discount_percent | default: 5

  comment
    Rounded to the rupee, and to the nearest one rather than up or down.

    Five percent of Rs. 1,299 is Rs. 64.95, and printing the paise is false
    precision in two directions at once: there has been no sub-rupee tender in
    circulation here for years, and the figure is per-unit anyway, since the
    code applies to the cart and a shopper who takes two saves twice this. A
    round number is the honest register for what a shopper keeps, and the code
    and the percentage are both on screen for anyone who wants to check it.

    Assumes a two-decimal currency, which this store is. In a zero-decimal
    currency, adding 50 would round to the nearest hundred rather than to the
    nearest unit, so this is the line to revisit if such a market is added.

    floor is written out rather than left to divided_by. Shopify's divided_by
    truncates when both sides are integers, which is the only reason this
    returns whole rupees at all — an undeclared dependency on the types of two
    values this file does not own. Stated explicitly it produces exactly the
    same figure and stops being a trick: without it, a price that ever arrives
    as a float prints Rs. 65.45 in a slot designed for Rs. 65.
  endcomment
  assign prepaid_exact = v.price | times: prepaid_pct | divided_by: 100 | floor
  assign prepaid_saving = prepaid_exact | plus: 50 | divided_by: 100 | floor | times: 100

  comment
    The urgency line, and the only two things it is ever allowed to say.

    A discount that is actually configured on the variant, or a dispatch
    promise the merchant has actually entered. Nothing else: no countdown, no
    stock figure, no shopper count, no deadline. Each of those would have to be
    invented here, because the theme has no source for any of them — and an
    invented one is a claim a customer can catch, on a store selling to
    parents, for a few percent of lift at most.

    The percentage is the same expression as the pill in snippets/price.liquid,
    deliberately, on the same two variant fields in the same order. It has to
    truncate the way the pill does, or the page would state two different
    discounts one line apart. If that expression ever changes, this one changes
    with it. The trailing floor is what the pill gets for free from integer
    division and is written out here for the same reason as above: on Shopify
    it is the identical 31, and without it a float price would print a
    seventeen-digit percentage in red at the top of the page.
  endcomment
  assign saved_pct = 0
  if v.compare_at_price > v.price
    assign saved_pct = v.compare_at_price | minus: v.price | times: 100 | divided_by: v.compare_at_price | floor
  endif

  assign urgency = ''
  if saved_pct > 0
    assign urgency = saved_pct | append: '% OFF &middot; Limited-time price'
  elsif settings.dispatch_promise != blank
    assign urgency = settings.dispatch_promise
  endif
-%}
<div class="buybox {% if compact %}buybox--compact{% endif %}" data-buybox id="buybox">

  {%- unless compact -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      No product.type eyebrow. It printed "PLAY MAT" directly above "MamaJoy
      Baby Play Mat – Toys & Cars", so it repeated a word the heading already
      carried and cost 36px of the one screen where the name and the price
      both have to be readable. A category label earns its place when it says
      something the title does not; this one did not.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <h1 class="buybox-title">{{ product.title }}</h1>
    {%- comment -%}
      Evidence sits between the name and the price, the order the questions
      actually get asked in: what is it, is it any good, what does it cost.
      Same snippet and same scope as the product card, so it speaks only for
      this product and renders nothing at all until it has a review of its own
      to report — which is the state every product is in today.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'product-rating', product: product %}
  {%- endunless -%}

  <div class="buybox-price-row">
    {% render 'price', variant: v %}
    <span class="buybox-tax-note">Inclusive of taxes</span>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Under the price, above the offer. This is the answer to "why now", and it
    belongs after the shopper knows the number and before they are asked to
    act on it — a reason to hurry that arrives after the button has already
    been offered is a nag rather than a reason.

    Red, and the only red on the page. --danger is otherwise reserved for
    things that have gone wrong, which is exactly what earns it attention
    here; it is 6.06:1 on chalk, so it passes as body text and not merely as a
    decoration. One line, 13px, medium — a step below the price it qualifies
    and two below the button it points at. It does not blink, count down,
    animate or fill a box, because every one of those reads as a marketplace
    listing rather than as a brand, and this store's whole argument is that it
    is not one.

    It says nothing at all when there is nothing true to say. No sale on the
    variant and no dispatch promise entered means no element, no empty space
    and no placeholder.

    Full buy box only. The compact one already drops the title and the rating;
    on the PDP it is the closing module, and the same red line twice on one
    page is how a true statement starts sounding like a technique.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if urgency != blank -%}
    {%- unless compact -%}
      <p class="buybox-urgency">{{ urgency }}</p>
    {%- endunless -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- form 'product', product, data-buybox-form: '' -%}
    <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ v.id }}" data-variant-id>

    {%- comment -%}
      The offer, before the quantity rather than under the button. A shopper
      decides how to pay while they are still deciding what to pay, and an
      incentive introduced after the primary action has already been offered is
      an upsell; introduced before it, it is a reason.

      It states rupees, not a percentage. 5% is a number to be evaluated;
      "save Rs. 65" is a number to be taken, and it is multiplied out of the
      live price and the configured percentage so it can never disagree with
      what the code actually does at checkout.

      One tap really is one tap: the shared handler adds the item, then sends
      the shopper to /discount/CODE?redirect=/checkout, which is Shopify's own
      mechanism for applying a code without the shopper typing it. The code is
      shown rather than hidden behind the offer, because a saving whose shape a
      shopper cannot see reads as a trick.

      Below 900px this is the only visible button in the panel, so leaving for
      checkout has to be disclosed rather than sprung. The arrow does that for
      anyone who can see it; the sentence after the code does it for anyone who
      cannot, which is why the caption is four words on screen and a full one
      in the accessible name.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if prepaid_code != blank and prepaid_saving > 0 and v.available -%}
      <button type="button" class="prepaid" data-buy-prepaid data-discount-code="{{ prepaid_code }}">
        <span class="btn-label prepaid-body">
          <span class="prepaid-main">Pay online &amp; save {{ prepaid_saving | money_without_trailing_zeros }}</span>
          {%- comment -%}
            The visible caption is four words and an arrow, because the rupee
            figure above it is the message and this line only has to say how to
            get it. The arrow is the disclosure: this control leaves the page.

            The sentence a screen reader hears is longer on purpose. Sighted
            shoppers have the arrow, the code and the position of the button
            on the page to tell them this is a shortcut to checkout rather than
            a second Add to Cart; a caption read out on its own has none of
            that, and "apply in 1 tap" alone would not say that the tap also
            adds the item and leaves. The visible copy and the accessible name
            describe the same action — one of them just has more room.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="prepaid-sub">
            <b class="prepaid-code">{{ prepaid_code }}</b>
            <span aria-hidden="true">&middot; Apply in 1 tap &rarr;</span>
            <span class="visually-hidden">is applied for you. Adds this item and continues to checkout.</span>
          </span>
        </span>
        <span class="btn-pending" aria-hidden="true">Redirecting&hellip;</span>
      </button>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="qty-row">
      <span class="qty-label" id="qtyLabel-{{ section.id }}">Quantity</span>
      <div class="stepper" data-qty-stepper>
        <button type="button" class="qty-btn" data-qty-minus aria-label="Decrease quantity">{% render 'icon', name: 'minus', size: 16 %}</button>
        <input type="number" class="qty-input" name="quantity" value="1" min="1" max="10" step="1" inputmode="numeric" data-qty-input aria-labelledby="qtyLabel-{{ section.id }}">
        <button type="button" class="qty-btn" data-qty-plus aria-label="Increase quantity">{% render 'icon', name: 'plus', size: 16 %}</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Same pending pattern as the product card: both words ship and CSS shows
      one, keyed off the aria-busy the shared handler sets. No spinner.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <button type="submit" class="cta cta-block" data-add-to-cart {% unless v.available %}disabled{% endunless %}>
      {%- if v.available -%}
        <span class="btn-label">Add to Cart</span>
        <span class="btn-pending" aria-hidden="true">Adding&hellip;</span>
      {%- else -%}Sold out{%- endif -%}
    </button>

    <p class="buybox-error" data-buybox-error role="alert" hidden></p>
  {%- endform -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Four facts, each one true and each one already promised elsewhere on the
    site: the announcement bar, the FAQ and the shipping accordion all say the
    same things. No countdown, no visitor count, no invented stock figure —
    the only urgency here is the kind that survives a shopper checking it.

    A list rather than a sentence, because these are four separate claims and a
    screen reader should be able to count them. The tick is the theme's own
    icon, marked decorative: the words already carry the meaning and a repeated
    "check" announcement four times over is noise.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <ul class="buybox-trust">
    <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 14 %}Cash on delivery</li>
    <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 14 %}Free shipping</li>
    <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 14 %}30-day returns</li>
    <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 14 %}Secure checkout</li>
  </ul>
</div>
